David Ernst

Usage:
    plex-letterboxd-sync [options] <export.zip|account>
Argument:
    <export.zip|account>
                        A Letterboxd data export (Settings > Data > Export),
                        which holds everything the account has logged - or the
                        account's name, whose public RSS feed holds only the
                        fifty or so most recent diary entries.
Environment:
    plexUrl             The Plex server, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:32400
    plexToken           Its X-Plex-Token.
Options:
    -s, --sections <names>
                    Only the Plex movie libraries with these titles,
                    comma-separated. Default: every movie library.
    -l, --to-plex-only
                    Letterboxd -> Plex only: mark films played and rated, and
                    write no CSV.
    -o, --output <file>
                    Write the CSV for Letterboxd's importer to <file>. Default:
                    logs/letterboxd-import-<date>.csv beside this script.
    -k, --state <file>
                    Remember what Letterboxd has, and what was sent to it, in
                    <file>, and keep a copy of the Plex library beside it, so
                    the next run reads only what changed. Default:
                    data/plex-letterboxd-state.json beside this script.
    -a, --account <id>
                    Read the plays of the Plex account with this number on the
                    server rather than its owner's (1). The numbers are listed
                    at <plexUrl>/accounts.
    -f, --full      Read the whole Plex library rather than what changed since the
                    last run - which a run also does by itself once a week, to
                    see films removed or marked unplayed.
    -p, --preview   Preview: say what would be marked, rated, added and
                    exported, and change nothing - not Plex, not the CSVs, not
                    the state file. The copy of the Plex library is kept.
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -v, --verbose   Also say why each item was handled the way it was, and on a
                    failure print the tool's whole output rather than its last
                    lines.
    -q, --quiet     Print errors only - no progress and no warnings.
